checks/audits/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000-2026-10-03-r3-editor.md:26:1. **Minor · residual · CLARIFY.** Page: `USA Today headlined the release “September jobs report shows slowdown in hiring, unemployment ticks up”.` The response tests a *payroll change*, while the sentence that says September *gross hires* are unpublished comes much later. Put the net-versus-gross distinction here in reader language. The author’s ledger records the [JOLTS admission](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/checks/working-notes/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.md:722); the connection remains for readers to make.
checks/working-notes/jobs--2026-10-02-september-payrolls-rise-29000.md:744:Cause of the 29,000 payroll change: the September employment release states the change and says it changed little. It does not give a reason for the size. The Associated Press says unemployment rose partly because 485,000 people entered the workforce. That sentence is about the rate, and the household table shows the same labor-force increase. The St. Louis Fed note gives flows behind the rate's few hundredths. CBS writes that the payroll figure signals businesses holding off amid energy prices and inflation. That is the outlet's characterization, not a reason the release states. The absence sentence names the September employment release.

