**Critical · new.** **Page bytes:** “Neither half is a change the bureau itself reports: its summary says payrolls and the unemployment rate both ‘changed little’.”

**Record bytes:** The pinned [BLS release](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/bls-empsit-2026-09.txt:339) reports `| Unemployment rate | 4.4 | 4.1 | 4.1 | 4.2 | 0.1 |`. The pinned [St. Louis Fed note](/Volumes/4/GitHub/the-catch-site-wt-jobs-sept/data/sources/stlouisfed-unrate-2026-10.txt:17) says, “the headline unemployment rate ticked up to 4.2% in September.”

**Correct account:** The BLS did report a rise in the rounded unemployment rate from 4.1 to 4.2 percent, while describing the change as small. The payroll figure measures net jobs, so it does not establish a slowdown in gross hiring.

